prompt:
Use Three.js to implement an interactive 3D visualization of Fourier series approximation, showing how rotating circles (epicycles) combine to form a target waveform.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Target Waveform Selection]
(1) A dropdown selector with id="waveform", with options: square (square wave), sawtooth (sawtooth wave), triangle (triangle wave), and custom (custom, allowing combinations such as 2*sin(t)+sin(3t));
(2) Compute the corresponding Fourier series coefficients according to the selection:
  - Square wave: bn = 4/(nπ), n=1,3,5,... (odd terms)
  - Sawtooth wave: bn = 2*(-1)^(n+1)/(nπ), n=1,2,3,...
  - Triangle wave: bn = 8*(-1)^((n-1)/2)/(n²π²), n=1,3,5,... (odd terms)
  - custom: use a preset set of coefficients [{amplitude, frequency, phase},...]

[Harmonic Term Count Control]
(3) A slider with id="nTerms", range 1-50, with a default value of 1;
(4) When adjusted, update the number of circles and the waveform approximation accuracy in real time;

[3D Epicycle Visualization]
(5) Visualize N concentrically rotating circles (epicycles) in 3D space:
  - The 1st circle: its center is at the origin, its radius = the magnitude of the 1st Fourier coefficient, and it rotates at that term's frequency;
  - The kth circle: its center is at the circumference endpoint of the k-1th circle, its radius = the magnitude of the kth coefficient, and its frequency = the kth term's harmonic frequency;
(6) Draw each circle using THREE.Line (the edge of CircleGeometry or a custom ring created with BufferGeometry), with a color gradient indicating harmonic order;
(7) Draw a radius line on each circle (from the circle center to the rotating endpoint), and mark the endpoint with a small sphere;
(8) The endpoint of the final circle is the current synthesized value;

[Waveform Trace]
(9) Extrude and draw the waveform curve along the Z-axis using the y component of the final circle endpoint's coordinates (or the synthesized value), producing an effect similar to an oscilloscope sweep;
(10) Use THREE.Line or THREE.TubeGeometry for the waveform curve, colored bright green or a custom color;
(11) The waveform extends in the positive Z direction over time; retain the most recent 500 sample points and remove older points;
(12) Include a horizontal reference line connecting the final endpoint to the starting point of the waveform curve to facilitate visual alignment;

[Coefficient Bar Chart Panel]
(13) Display a bar chart of the amplitudes of all current harmonic terms in a right-side panel (DOM overlay or CSS fixed positioning);
(14) The number of bars = nTerms; each bar's height is proportional to the magnitude of that term's coefficient, and its color matches the corresponding circle;
(15) Label the harmonic order (n=1, n=2, ...);

[Speed Control]
(16) A speed slider with id="speed", range 0.1-5.0, step 0.1, with a default value of 1.0;
(17) It affects the rotational animation and waveform sweep speed;

[Error Display]
(18) Calculate the L2 norm error between the current approximated waveform and the target waveform (using 256 discrete sample points over one complete period), and display it in the DOM element with id="errorDisplay";
(19) Format: "Error: 0.xxxx";

[Camera and Background]
(20) OrbitControls;
(21) Initial view: a 45° oblique view that can show both the circles and the waveform curve simultaneously;
(22) Dark background;
(23) Add a GridHelper or reference plane to aid spatial perception;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(24) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame, containing:
  - nTerms: number (current number of harmonic terms)
  - waveform: string (current waveform type: 'square'|'sawtooth'|'triangle'|'custom')
  - speed: number (current speed)
  - error: number (L2 norm error)
  - circles: Array<{radius: number, frequency: number, phase: number}> (parameters of each circle)
  - tracePointCount: number (current number of waveform trace sample points)
  - simTime: number (simulation time in seconds)

Output a single HTML file that can run by being opened directly in a browser.
